(window.CLART=window.CLART||{})["epistaxis"]={"h": "<section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c1\" data-t=\"Summary\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Summary</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">A common bleed with an uncommon tail.</p><p>Epistaxis is bleeding from inside the nose. The blood may run forward out of the nostril, backward into the nasopharynx and throat, or both. It is always a sign of something else, never a final diagnosis, and one rule holds on every shift: an attempt should always be made to find its local or systemic cause. About nine in ten bleeds start anteriorly in Little's area and stop with first aid or cautery; posterior bleeds in older, hypertensive or anticoagulated patients are a resuscitation problem as much as a nasal one. Treatment climbs a ladder: first aid, cautery, anterior packing, posterior packing, then endoscopic cautery, ligation or embolisation.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c2\" data-t=\"Epidemiology\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Epidemiology</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Bimodal.</p><p>Most people have at least one nosebleed in their lives, and around one in six of the population is estimated to bleed in any given year. Only a small fraction ever seek medical help. The distribution is bimodal: children and young adults, who bleed from the front of the septum, and adults past middle age, who make up most hospital admissions and most posterior bleeds. The patients who reach the emergency department are older, often hypertensive, frequently on an anticoagulant or antiplatelet drug, and bleeding from further back in the nose, where blood runs silently down the pharynx and is easy to underestimate. In them, epistaxis is as much a resuscitation problem as a nasal one.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c3\" data-t=\"Etiology\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Etiology</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Local, systemic, or neither.</p><div class=\"artree\"><span class=\"root\">Causes of epistaxis</span><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Local</span><b>Trauma</b> <span>inflammation and drying, structural, neoplastic, iatrogenic</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Systemic</span><b>Drugs</b> <span>coagulation and platelet disorders, vascular disorders, cardiovascular, organ failure</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Idiopathic</span><b>No cause found</b> <span>despite assessment</span></li></ul></div><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.1</span><h3>Local causes</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-1-1\"><h4><i>3.1.1</i>Trauma</h4><p>The commonest cause overall. Digital trauma (nose picking) dominates in children; in adults add forceful blowing, blows to the face, fractures of the nasal bones, midface and skull base, and foreign bodies.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-1-2\"><h4><i>3.1.2</i>Inflammation and drying</h4><p>Dry, heated air in winter, acute viral rhinitis and sinusitis, rhinitis sicca, atrophic rhinitis, and the crust-forming granulomatous infections all leave a fragile, cracked mucosa.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-1-3\"><h4><i>3.1.3</i>Structural</h4><p>A septal spur or deviation creates turbulent, drying airflow over its edge; a septal perforation bleeds from its crusted rim.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-1-4\"><h4><i>3.1.4</i>Neoplastic</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Benign:</b> juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma, haemangioma, inverted papilloma.</li><li><b>Malignant:</b> carcinoma of the nose, sinuses and nasopharynx.</li></ul><p>Any unilateral, recurrent bleed, especially with obstruction, must be examined endoscopically.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-1-5\"><h4><i>3.1.5</i>Iatrogenic and topical</h4><p>Nasal and sinus surgery, nasogastric and nasotracheal tubes, nasal oxygen cannulae, topical steroid and decongestant sprays, and intranasal cocaine.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.2</span><h3>Systemic causes</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The logic is simple: anything that impairs clotting, platelets or vessel walls will show up in the nose.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-2-1\"><h4><i>3.2.1</i>Drugs and supplements</h4><p>Warfarin and the direct oral anticoagulants, aspirin, clopidogrel and other antiplatelets, NSAIDs, and over-the-counter vitamin E, fish oil, garlic, ginkgo and ginseng.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-2-2\"><h4><i>3.2.2</i>Coagulation and platelet disorders</h4><p>Haemophilia, von Willebrand disease (the commonest inherited bleeding disorder), thrombocytopenia, leukaemia and aplastic anaemia.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-2-3\"><h4><i>3.2.3</i>Vascular disorders</h4><p>Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-hht\">Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia</button>), scurvy, and the vessel fragility of old age.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-2-4\"><h4><i>3.2.4</i>Cardiovascular</h4><p>Hypertension and atherosclerosis are associated with posterior bleeds in older adults. Whether raised pressure causes the bleed or simply keeps it going is still debated; either way, it should be measured and treated.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b3-2-5\"><h4><i>3.2.5</i>Organ failure</h4><p>Liver failure (reduced clotting factors) and renal failure (uraemic platelet dysfunction).</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.3</span><h3>Idiopathic</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>In a large share of patients no specific cause is found despite a careful history, examination and blood tests. The label is earned only after the search, not instead of it.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f1\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-3\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 1\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-3.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 1</b>Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia: lesions on the nasal septum, tongue, palate and fingertips (arrows). Always look at the lips, tongue and fingers of a recurrent bleeder.<cite>Dhingra, fig. 33.3</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-flag\"><h5>Red flags</h5><ul><li><b>Adolescent boy, unilateral bleeding and obstruction:</b> juvenile angiofibroma until proven otherwise (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-juv\">Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</button>). No biopsy in clinic.</li><li><b>Adult with unilateral bleeding, obstruction, crusting or facial pain:</b> endoscopy for a sinonasal tumour (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-sinus_ca\">Sinonasal tumours</button>).</li><li><b>A child under two:</b> rare; think of non-accidental injury or a bleeding disorder.</li><li><b>Massive or recurrent bleeding after trauma or skull base surgery:</b> carotid pseudoaneurysm; needs angiography.</li></ul></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c4\" data-t=\"Pathophysiology\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Pathophysiology</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Two carotid systems meeting in the nose.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.1</span><h3>Arterial supply</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>The nasal cavity draws blood from both the internal and the external carotid arteries, and the two systems anastomose freely on the septum and the lateral wall. That double supply is why nosebleeds can be persistent, and why ligating one vessel sometimes isn't enough.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-1-1\"><h4><i>4.1.1</i>Internal carotid system</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Anterior ethmoidal artery,</b> from the ophthalmic artery, entering the nose through the roof.</li><li><b>Posterior ethmoidal artery,</b> also from the ophthalmic, smaller and further back.</li></ul><p>These supply the upper septum and lateral wall above the middle turbinate.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-1-2\"><h4><i>4.1.2</i>External carotid system</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Sphenopalatine artery,</b> terminal branch of the maxillary artery: the main supply of the nose.</li><li><b>Greater palatine artery,</b> from the maxillary, reaching the septum through the incisive canal.</li><li><b>Superior labial artery,</b> from the facial, supplying the front of the septum and vestibule.</li></ul><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f2\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-1\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-1.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-2\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 2</b>Arterial supply of the nasal septum (first) and the lateral nasal wall (second). The anterior ethmoidal, septal branch of the sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial arteries converge on Little's area.<cite>Dhingra, figs 33.1 and 33.2</cite></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"arfig arapp\" id=\"a-f3\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"nd104_14\" data-set=\"nd104_14\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 3\"><img src=\"fig2/t/nd104_14.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 3</b>The ophthalmic artery giving off the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries in the orbit, on their way to the roof of the nose.<cite data-figcite=\"nd104_14\">From the anatomy atlas</cite></figcaption></figure></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.2</span><h3>Little's area and Kiesselbach's plexus</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>On the anteroinferior septum, just above the vestibule, four arteries meet to form <em class=\"term\">Kiesselbach's plexus</em>; the region it occupies is <em class=\"term\">Little's area</em>.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-2-1\"><h4><i>4.2.1</i>The four contributors</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li>Anterior ethmoidal (internal carotid)</li><li>Septal branch of the sphenopalatine (external carotid)</li><li>Greater palatine (external carotid)</li><li>Septal branch of the superior labial (external carotid)</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-2-2\"><h4><i>4.2.2</i>Why it bleeds</h4><p>It sits exactly where inspired air dries and cracks the mucosa, where the mucosa is thin and closely bound to cartilage, and where a fingernail reaches. About nine in ten bleeds start here.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.3</span><h3>The posterior territory</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-3-1\"><h4><i>4.3.1</i>Sphenopalatine artery</h4><p>It enters the nose through the sphenopalatine foramen, just behind and below the <em class=\"term\">crista ethmoidalis</em> of the palatine bone, near the posterior end of the middle turbinate, and often divides into several branches before emerging. It is the dominant supply to the nose in about 85% of people (the rest are anterior ethmoid-dominant). Bleeding from its posterior lateral nasal and posterior septal branches is the classic posterior epistaxis.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-3-2\"><h4><i>4.3.2</i>Woodruff's plexus</h4><p>A plexus on the lateral wall below the posterior end of the inferior turbinate, and the other classic posterior bleeding site in adults.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b4-3-3\"><h4><i>4.3.3</i>Ethmoidal territory</h4><p>Bleeding above the level of the middle turbinate points to the ethmoidal arteries and therefore to the internal carotid system, which matters because embolisation cannot safely reach it.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-differ\"><h5>Where the books differ · Woodruff&#x27;s plexus</h5><div class=\"cols\"><div><b>Dhingra</b>Woodruff's plexus is a plexus of veins below the posterior end of the inferior turbinate.</div><div><b>Lee</b>Woodruff's is the naso-nasopharyngeal plexus over the posterior centimetre of the nasal floor, inferior meatus and middle meatus.</div><div><b>In practice</b>For the exam, give the first definition, and add that posterior arterial bleeds come from sphenopalatine branches.</div></div></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.4</span><h3>Venous drainage</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Veins follow the arteries. The <em class=\"term\">retrocolumellar vein</em>, running vertically just behind the columella across the floor of the nose, is a common source of venous bleeding in young people.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c5\" data-t=\"Diagnosis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Diagnosis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Resuscitate first, then find the bleeding point, then ask why.</p><p>The first question is not where the blood is coming from, but how much has been lost and whether the airway is safe. A posterior bleed can fill the stomach before it fills a basin, and swallowed blood that returns as dark vomit is easily mistaken for haematemesis. The first five minutes are set out in <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-s7-1\">Approach</button>.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.1</span><h3>History</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-1-1\"><h4><i>5.1.1</i>The bleed</h4><p>Side of onset, duration, estimated volume, whether it runs forward or into the throat, and previous episodes and their treatment.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-1-2\"><h4><i>5.1.2</i>Local symptoms</h4><p>Obstruction, discharge, crusting, facial pain or numbness, and trauma.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-1-3\"><h4><i>5.1.3</i>Systemic background</h4><p>Medications including supplements, hypertension, liver or renal disease, easy bruising, and a family history of bleeding or nosebleeds.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.2</span><h3>Examination</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-2-1\"><h4><i>5.2.1</i>General</h4><p>Pallor, pulse and blood pressure, signs of liver disease, bruising, and telangiectasias on the lips, tongue and fingertips.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-2-2\"><h4><i>5.2.2</i>Anterior rhinoscopy</h4><p>After clot removal and vasoconstriction: inspect Little's area first, then the front of the lateral wall. The oropharynx is checked for blood running down from behind.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-2-3\"><h4><i>5.2.3</i>Nasal endoscopy</h4><p>For recurrent bleeds, for bleeding not localised on rhinoscopy, and for any unilateral bleed with suspicious symptoms. It finds points hidden lateral to the turbinates or high in the nose and allows directed cautery instead of a blind pack.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.3</span><h3>Investigations</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-3-1\"><h4><i>5.3.1</i>Blood tests</h4><p>Not needed for a minor anterior bleed in a healthy patient. For significant bleeds: full blood count, group and save or crossmatch, clotting with INR in warfarinised patients, and liver and renal function where relevant.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-3-2\"><h4><i>5.3.2</i>Imaging</h4><p>CT or MRI when a tumour is suspected; for juvenile angiofibroma, contrast imaging replaces biopsy.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b5-3-3\"><h4><i>5.3.3</i>Angiography</h4><p>Diagnostic and therapeutic: for suspected pseudoaneurysm, vascular tumours, and as the first step of embolisation.</p><div class=\"arcases\" data-arcases=\"\" data-lead=\"Juvenile angiofibroma, the tumour to think of in a teenage boy with one-sided bleeding.\"></div></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.4</span><h3>Investigations: what and when</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Epistaxis: investigations</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Role</th><th scope=\"col\">Test</th><th scope=\"col\">When and what it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First (every patient)</td><td>Pulse, blood pressure; anterior rhinoscopy after clearing clot</td><td>Shock; anterior bleeding point</td></tr><tr><td>Locate a posterior bleed</td><td>Rigid nasal endoscopy</td><td>Bleeding point for targeted cautery</td></tr><tr><td>Significant bleed</td><td>Full blood count, group and save or crossmatch</td><td>Anaemia; need for transfusion</td></tr><tr><td>On warfarin or suspected coagulopathy</td><td>INR and clotting screen; liver function</td><td>Over-anticoagulation, liver disease</td></tr><tr><td>Recurrent bleeds, bruising, family history</td><td>Clotting screen with von Willebrand studies</td><td>Bleeding disorder</td></tr><tr><td>Unilateral bleeding with obstruction, teenage boy</td><td>Contrast CT and MRI (no biopsy)</td><td>Juvenile angiofibroma, tumour</td></tr><tr><td>After trauma, or refractory bleeding</td><td>CT angiography or catheter angiography</td><td>Pseudoaneurysm; embolisation target</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c6\" data-t=\"Subtypes and variants\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Subtypes and variants</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Site, severity and pattern; special groups.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.1</span><h3>Anterior or posterior</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>With the patient sitting up, an anterior bleed runs mostly out of the nostril. A posterior bleed runs back into the throat, often from both sides, and no source is seen on anterior rhinoscopy.</p><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\">Anterior</th><th scope=\"col\">Posterior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Frequency</th><td>Common, about 90%</td><td>Less common</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Site</th><td>Little's area or the front of the lateral wall</td><td>Posterosuperior cavity; often hard to localise</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Age</th><td>Children and young adults</td><td>Usually over 40</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Cause</th><td>Mostly trauma, dryness</td><td>Often spontaneous; hypertension, atherosclerosis, anticoagulation</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Severity</th><td>Usually mild; pressure, cautery or an anterior pack</td><td>Often severe; admission, posterior pack or surgery</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.2</span><h3>By severity</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>There is no universal scale; in practice the useful split is whether first aid is enough.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-2-1\"><h4><i>6.2.1</i>Minor</h4><p>Stops with pressure or a single cautery, haemodynamically stable, can go home.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-2-2\"><h4><i>6.2.2</i>Major</h4><p>Continues despite first aid, needs packing, shows haemodynamic change or a falling haemoglobin, or occurs in a frail or anticoagulated patient. These patients are admitted.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.3</span><h3>Single or recurrent</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Recurrence changes the question from how to stop it to why it keeps happening. Recurrent unilateral bleeding needs endoscopy; recurrent bilateral bleeding, especially with a family history, needs HHT to be considered.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.4</span><h3>Children</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-4-1\"><h4><i>6.4.1</i>Typical picture</h4><p>Bleeding from Little's area, from crusting and picking, that stops on its own.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-4-2\"><h4><i>6.4.2</i>Treatment</h4><p>Antiseptic cream such as chlorhexidine with neomycin to the vestibule twice daily for four weeks settles most cases; silver nitrate for a visible vessel that keeps bleeding. Avoid creams containing arachis oil in peanut allergy.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b6-4-3\"><h4><i>6.4.3</i>When to investigate</h4><p>Recurrent bleeding with easy bruising or a family history needs a clotting screen including von Willebrand studies. Under two years of age, think of a bleeding disorder or non-accidental injury.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.5</span><h3>The anticoagulated patient</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Patients on warfarin, a direct oral anticoagulant or dual antiplatelets bleed longer and re-bleed more often, but stopping their drugs risks stroke or stent thrombosis. Control the bleed locally first with resorbable packing and check the INR in warfarinised patients. Reversal and withholding are for life-threatening bleeds, decided with the prescribing team: for warfarin with major bleeding, intravenous vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate (fresh frozen plasma only if that is unavailable); for a high INR with minor bleeding, withhold warfarin and give oral vitamin K. Ask about \"natural blood thinners\" such as garlic, ginger, ginkgo, turmeric and vitamin E.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.6</span><h3>Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Recurrent bleeding from multiple telangiectasias, usually from childhood, with a family history. Repeated packing and aggressive cautery make things worse; laser, bevacizumab, septodermoplasty and Young's procedure are the tools. The full account is in <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-hht\">Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia</button>.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-7\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.7</span><h3>Trauma and pseudoaneurysm</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Delayed, massive or recurrent bleeding days to weeks after head injury or skull base surgery suggests a pseudoaneurysm of the internal carotid artery. It is a neurovascular emergency: pack, resuscitate and arrange urgent angiography.</p></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c7\" data-t=\"Management\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Management</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">The ladder of control.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.1</span><h3>Approach</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-ward\"><h5>On the ward: the first five minutes</h5><ol><li>Sit the patient up and leaning forward. Check pulse, blood pressure and saturation; secure IV access and send a full blood count, group and save, and clotting if anticoagulated or bleeding heavily.</li><li>Firm pressure on the soft lower third of the nose for at least five minutes without peeking; many units ask for 10 to 15. Ice in the mouth adds a little reflex vasoconstriction.</li><li>Suction the clots, spray topical vasoconstrictor and anaesthetic, and look with a headlight and speculum.</li><li>Ask about anticoagulants, antiplatelets, previous bleeds, family history and alcohol, and which side bled first.</li><li>Don't stop or reverse anticoagulants for a bleed that local measures can control. Discuss with the prescribing team if it is life-threatening.</li></ol></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.2</span><h3>The treatment ladder</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ol class=\"arlad\" style=\"--n:6\"><li><span class=\"dot\">1</span><div><b>First aid and topical vasoconstriction</b><span>Pressure on the soft nose, leaning forward; pledgets of vasoconstrictor and local anaesthetic.</span></div></li><li><span class=\"dot\">2</span><div><b>Cautery of a visible point</b><span>Silver nitrate or bipolar, to the bleeding point only.</span></div></li><li><span class=\"dot\">3</span><div><b>Anterior packing</b><span>Ribbon gauze, expanding sponge, or resorbable material.</span></div></li><li><span class=\"dot\">4</span><div><b>Posterior packing</b><span>Postnasal pack, Foley or dual balloon; admit and monitor.</span></div></li><li><span class=\"dot\">5</span><div><b>Endoscopic cautery</b><span>Directed at the source under local or general anaesthesia.</span></div></li><li><span class=\"dot\">6</span><div><b>Ligation or embolisation</b><span>For bleeding that persists despite packing.</span></div></li></ol></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.3</span><h3>First aid</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p><em class=\"term\">Trotter's method</em>: the patient sits up, leaning slightly forward over a basin, breathing through the mouth, while the soft part of the nose is pinched firmly against the septum. Leaning forward stops blood being swallowed and lets you see how much is lost. Sucking ice may add a little reflex vasoconstriction; ice packs on the nasal bridge have no proven benefit.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.4</span><h3>Cautery</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Cautery works when you can see what you are treating. Anaesthetise first, and treat only the bleeding point.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-4-1\"><h4><i>7.4.1</i>Chemical</h4><p>Roll a silver nitrate stick over a small ring around the vessel, edge first and then inwards, before touching the vessel itself. Best for small, visible anterior points in a field that is not actively pouring.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-4-2\"><h4><i>7.4.2</i>Electrocautery</h4><p>Bipolar diathermy, usually under endoscopic view, is more effective for brisker or deeper points and for posterior sites. Where available it is preferred to silver nitrate, because it fails less often and reduces packing, admission and pain. After silver nitrate, wipe or irrigate the vestibule with saline to prevent skin burns.</p><div class=\"arbox k-pearl\"><h5>Pearl</h5><p>Never cauterise both sides of the septum at the same sitting: the mucoperichondrium on both sides may necrose and leave a perforation.</p></div></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.5</span><h3>Anterior packing</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-5-1\"><h4><i>7.5.1</i>Ribbon gauze</h4><p>About a metre of ribbon gauze, 2.5 cm wide in adults and 12 mm in children, soaked in liquid paraffin or bismuth iodoform paraffin paste and laid in layers so that it presses evenly on the whole cavity. Usually removed after 24 hours; antibiotics are given if it is left longer.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-5-2\"><h4><i>7.5.2</i>Expanding sponges and balloons</h4><p>Compressed sponges that swell when wetted, and inflatable balloon packs: quicker to insert, more comfortable, and now the standard in most emergency departments.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-5-3\"><h4><i>7.5.3</i>Resorbable packs</h4><p>Preferred when the patient is anticoagulated or has a bleeding disorder, because removing a non-resorbable pack can restart the bleed.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f4\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-4\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 4\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-4.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 4</b>Anterior nasal packing. (A) Vertical layers from the back forwards. (B) Horizontal layers from the floor upwards.<cite>Dhingra, fig. 33.4</cite></figcaption></figure></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.6</span><h3>Posterior packing</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>When blood runs back into the pharynx and an anterior pack fails, the choana must be blocked.</p><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-6-1\"><h4><i>7.6.1</i>Postnasal gauze pack</h4><p>A rolled gauze cone with three silk ties is drawn into the nasopharynx on a catheter passed through the nose and out of the mouth, then pushed into place with a finger. An anterior pack goes in front, and two ties are secured over a dental roll at the columella. The third tie is left in the oropharynx, taped to the cheek, to pull the pack out later.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-6-2\"><h4><i>7.6.2</i>Foley catheter</h4><p>A 12 to 14 F Foley is passed until the balloon lies in the nasopharynx, inflated with saline (not air), drawn forward against the choana and backed by an anterior pack. Protect the ala and columella from pressure.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-6-3\"><h4><i>7.6.3</i>Dual-balloon devices</h4><p>Commercial catheters with a posterior and an anterior balloon, and a central tube that preserves a nasal airway.</p><figure class=\"arfig\" id=\"a-f5\"><div class=\"pair\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-5\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 5\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-5.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-arfig=\"d33-6\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 5\"><img src=\"clinic/fig/d33-6.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 5</b>First, inserting a postnasal pack on a catheter. Second, a dual-balloon catheter with the posterior balloon at about 10 mL and the anterior at about 30 mL.<cite>Dhingra, figs 33.5 and 33.6</cite></figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-6-4\"><h4><i>7.6.4</i>Care of the packed patient</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Admit and monitor:</b> posterior packs fail in about a third.</li><li><b>Oxygen:</b> bilateral packs can cause hypoxia through the nasopulmonary reflex, especially in the elderly.</li><li><b>Watch for:</b> alar and columellar necrosis, sinusitis, otitis media, aspiration and toxic shock syndrome.</li><li><b>Antibiotics</b> are traditionally given while a non-resorbable pack is in, though the evidence for routine use is weak.</li><li><b>Explain the pack:</b> what type it is, when it comes out, and what should bring them back.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-7\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.7</span><h3>Medical adjuncts</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-7-1\"><h4><i>7.7.1</i>Blood pressure</h4><p>Treat pain and anxiety first; control persistent hypertension, but avoid dropping the pressure sharply.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-7-2\"><h4><i>7.7.2</i>Tranexamic acid</h4><p>Topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for packing in the NoPAC trial; it should not delay standard treatment. Some smaller trials suggest a benefit, especially in patients on antiplatelet drugs, and oral tranexamic acid helps in HHT.</p><div class=\"arbox k-update\"><h5>Current evidence</h5><p>The American nosebleed guideline (Tunkel et al., <em class=\"term\">Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg</em> 2020) recommends compression for at least five minutes, packing if the site cannot be seen, resorbable packs in patients on anticoagulants or antiplatelets, nasal endoscopy for recurrent or difficult bleeds, cautery confined to the bleeding site, ligation or embolisation when these fail, and local treatment before transfusion, reversal or stopping anticoagulants unless the bleed is life-threatening. In the NoPAC trial of 496 patients in UK emergency departments, topical tranexamic acid did not reduce the need for anterior packing (43.7% vs 41.3% with placebo) (Reuben et al., <em class=\"term\">Ann Emerg Med</em> 2021). A UK national audit of 1,826 cases found 30-day re-presentation in 13.9% and all-cause 30-day mortality of 3.4%, and wide variation from consensus guidance (National ENT Trainee Research Network, <em class=\"term\">J Laryngol Otol</em> 2017).</p></div></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-7-3\"><h4><i>7.7.3</i>Drugs and doses</h4><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Epistaxis: drugs and doses</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Drug and dose</th><th scope=\"col\">Course and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before cautery or packing</td><td>Topical anaesthetic with vasoconstrictor: co-phenylcaine (lidocaine 5% with phenylephrine 0.5%) spray, or 1:1000 adrenaline on a pledget</td><td>Wait 3 to 4 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Cautery</td><td>Silver nitrate stick for 3 to 10 seconds to a visible point</td><td>One side of the septum at a time</td></tr><tr><td>After cautery or for recurrent anterior bleeds</td><td>Chlorhexidine–neomycin (Naseptin) cream to the nostrils four times a day for 10 days (twice a day for up to 2 weeks if needed)</td><td>Older stock contains peanut oil: check the formulation</td></tr><tr><td>Peanut, soya or neomycin allergy</td><td>Mupirocin 2% nasal ointment two or three times a day</td><td>5 to 7 days</td></tr><tr><td>Packing in place</td><td>Pack coated with antibiotic ointment; routine systemic antibiotics are not needed for anterior packs</td><td>Posterior packs: admit and monitor</td></tr><tr><td>Tranexamic acid (adjunct)</td><td>Oral 1 g three times a day; topically, the injection (100 mg/ml) on a pledget, for example 500 mg as in smaller trials</td><td>Oral for up to about 4 days; topical (2 ml) did not reduce packing in the NoPAC trial</td></tr><tr><td>Warfarin with major bleed</td><td>Vitamin K 5 to 10 mg IV with prothrombin complex concentrate, per local haematology protocol</td><td>Do not stop anticoagulants routinely for a minor bleed</td></tr><tr><td>DOAC with life-threatening bleed</td><td>Idarucizumab for dabigatran; andexanet alfa or prothrombin complex for factor Xa inhibitors, per haematology</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hypertension</td><td>Analgesia and calm first; treat persistent hypertension without a sharp fall</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Doses from NICE CKS Epistaxis, ENT UK guidance as used in NHS primary care pathways, the BNF and the NoPAC trial (Reuben et al., <em class=\"term\">Ann Emerg Med</em> 2021).</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-8\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.8</span><h3>Choosing the vessel</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artree\"><span class=\"root\">Persistent bleeding</span><ul><li><span class=\"k\">Source seen</span><b>Endoscopic cautery</b> <span>under local or general anaesthesia</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Posterior, below the middle turbinate</span><b>Sphenopalatine ligation</b> <span>or embolisation</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Above the middle turbinate</span><b>Ethmoidal ligation</b> <span>not embolisation</span></li><li><span class=\"k\">Septal deviation</span><b>Septal surgery</b> <span>to reach the bleed</span></li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-9\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.9</span><h3>Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-9-1\"><h4><i>7.9.1</i>Indications</h4><p>Posterior bleeding that persists or recurs despite adequate packing. TESPAL is now the operation of choice and has replaced the older open procedures.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-9-2\"><h4><i>7.9.2</i>Technique</h4><p>Under general anaesthesia, a mucosal incision on the lateral wall in front of the crista ethmoidalis; a mucoperiosteal flap is raised back to the crista, behind which the artery and its branches emerge from the foramen. They are clipped or coagulated, and the flap is replaced.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-9-3\"><h4><i>7.9.3</i>Outcomes and complications</h4><p>Control is reported in 95 to 98%, usually with discharge the next day; all branches emerging from the foramen must be controlled, and failure should prompt a search for an accessory branch or an ethmoidal source. Complications are minor: crusting, adhesions, epiphora and numbness of the palate or nose; visual loss is very rare.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-10\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.10</span><h3>Ethmoidal artery ligation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-10-1\"><h4><i>7.10.1</i>Approach</h4><p>An external (Lynch) incision at the medial canthus, raising the periosteum of the medial orbital wall, usually for high bleeds after trauma.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-10-2\"><h4><i>7.10.2</i>The 24-12-6 rule</h4><p>The anterior ethmoidal foramen lies about 24 mm behind the anterior lacrimal crest, the posterior foramen about 12 mm further back, and the optic canal about 6 mm beyond that. The posterior artery is approached with great care, or left alone.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-11\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.11</span><h3>Embolisation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-11-1\"><h4><i>7.11.1</i>Method</h4><p>The internal maxillary artery is catheterised by the femoral route and blocked with particles, gelfoam or coils, often bilaterally because of cross-flow.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-11-2\"><h4><i>7.11.2</i>Who it suits</h4><p>Patients unfit for anaesthesia, vascular tumours and traumatic pseudoaneurysms. It cannot treat the ethmoidal arteries, which arise from the internal carotid system.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-11-3\"><h4><i>7.11.3</i>Outcomes and risks</h4><p>Success around 88%. Complications include stroke, blindness, facial pain and skin necrosis.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-12\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.12</span><h3>Septal surgery</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Raising a mucoperichondrial flap divides the vessels feeding a recurrent septal bleed; septoplasty also straightens a deviation that hides a bleeding point or prevents adequate packing (<button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-septum\">Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess</button>).</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-13\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.13</span><h3>Historical operations</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-13-1\"><h4><i>7.13.1</i>Maxillary artery ligation</h4><p>Through a Caldwell-Luc approach and the posterior antral wall. Failure in 10 to 15%, with infraorbital numbness, oroantral fistula and dental injury.</p></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b7-13-2\"><h4><i>7.13.2</i>External carotid ligation</h4><p>Above the origin of the superior thyroid artery. Too proximal to be reliable, given the collateral flow; of examination interest now.</p><div class=\"arbox k-pearl\"><h5>Pearls</h5><ul><li>Bleeding above the middle turbinate is ethmoidal: embolisation won't help, think ligation.</li><li>The crista ethmoidalis is the landmark for the sphenopalatine foramen: the artery lies just behind it.</li><li>If endoscopic ligation fails, look for an ethmoidal source or a missed accessory branch before repacking.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"arops\" data-arops=\"\" data-lead=\"\"></div></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c8\" data-t=\"Complications\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Complications</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Of the bleed and of its treatment.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.1</span><h3>Of the bleed</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Hypovolaemic shock, anaemia, aspiration, and myocardial ischaemia in patients with coronary disease.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.2</span><h3>Of treatment</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Cautery:</b> septal perforation.</li><li><b>Packing:</b> pressure necrosis, sinusitis, otitis media, hypoxia, toxic shock syndrome.</li><li><b>Surgery and embolisation:</b> see <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-s7-9\">Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-s7-10\">Ethmoidal artery ligation</button> and <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx\" data-artgo=\"a-s7-11\">Embolisation</button>.</li></ul></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c9\" data-t=\"Prognosis\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Prognosis</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Recurrence and frailty.</p><p>Most bleeds settle with simple measures. A patient whose anterior bleed is stopped by cautery can go home with antiseptic or antibiotic cream for two weeks; after a significant posterior bleed, observe for 6 to 12 hours; anyone with a pack is admitted. Recurrence is common in the elderly, the anticoagulated and those with HHT, and patients admitted with epistaxis are often frail: in a UK national audit, 1 in 7 re-presented within 30 days and 3.4% died within 30 days of any cause.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c10\" data-t=\"Tips and links\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Tips and links</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">For the examination and the ward.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.1</span><h3>Viva essentials</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-exam\"><h5>What the examiner is looking for</h5><ul><li><b>Little's area:</b> the four arteries, their parent vessels, and why it bleeds.</li><li><b>Trotter's method:</b> position, mouth breathing, and pinching the soft nose.</li><li><b>Anterior versus posterior:</b> the five-row comparison.</li><li><b>Postnasal pack:</b> technique, the third tie, and why the patient is admitted.</li><li><b>Which artery for which site,</b> and why ethmoidal bleeds can't be embolised.</li><li><b>Teenage boy, unilateral recurrent bleed:</b> angiofibroma, and no biopsy in clinic.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.2</span><h3>Common pitfalls</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-pit\"><h5>Common pitfalls</h5><ul><li>Underestimating blood loss in an elderly patient who is swallowing blood.</li><li>Packing blindly before suctioning and looking, when a cauterisable point was visible.</li><li>Cauterising both sides of the septum at once.</li><li>Stopping anticoagulation reflexively for a bleed a pack would have controlled.</li><li>Repeated packing in recurrent bilateral bleeds that are really HHT.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s10-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">10.3</span><h3>Related topics</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Causes:</b> <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-hht\">Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-juv\">Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-sinus_ca\">Sinonasal tumours</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-septum\">Deviated septum, septal haematoma and abscess</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-facefx\">Nasal and facial fractures</button></li><li><b>Drugs:</b> <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-dr_anticoag\">Anticoagulants and antiplatelets around ENT surgery</button>, <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-dr_eacid\">Acidifying and astringent ear preparations</button></li><li><b>Approach:</b> <button type=\"button\" class=\"arx arxt\" data-go=\"cl:t-ap_rhin\">Approach to a runny nose</button></li></ul></div></details></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c amc\" id=\"a-c11\" data-t=\"Sources\"><details class=\"amch\"><summary><h2>Sources</h2><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"amchb\"><p class=\"arintro\">Where this topic comes from.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s11-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">11.1</span><h3>Books</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>Phillips and Bhalla, <em class=\"term\">Oxford Textbook of Otolaryngology</em> (2025), chapters 27 and 77.</li><li><em class=\"term\">Oxford Specialist Handbook of Otolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery</em>, 2nd edition, chapter 20.</li><li><em class=\"term\">Oxford Handbook of ENT and Head and Neck Surgery</em>, 3rd edition (2020), chapters 9 and 25.</li><li>Dhingra, <em class=\"term\">Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat</em>, chapter 33.</li><li>K. J. Lee, <em class=\"term\">Essential Otolaryngology</em>, chapter 31.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s11-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">11.2</span><h3>Studies cited</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>Tunkel et al., <em class=\"term\">Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg</em> 2020: American nosebleed guideline.</li><li>Reuben et al., <em class=\"term\">Ann Emerg Med</em> 2021: the NoPAC trial of topical tranexamic acid.</li><li>National ENT Trainee Research Network, <em class=\"term\">J Laryngol Otol</em> 2017: UK national epistaxis audit.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s11-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">11.3</span><h3>Guidelines and dosing</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>NICE CKS: Epistaxis (nosebleeds).</li><li>Reuben et al., <em class=\"term\">Ann Emerg Med</em> 2021: the NoPAC trial of tranexamic acid in epistaxis.</li><li>BNF: tranexamic acid, chlorhexidine–neomycin cream, mupirocin, co-phenylcaine.</li></ul></div></details></div></details></section>", "stand": "Bleeding from the nose is a sign, not a diagnosis. Most bleeds are small, anterior and self-limiting; a few are posterior, brisk and dangerous. The skill lies in telling the two apart, and in climbing the ladder of treatment one rung at a time.", "ch": ["Summary", "Epidemiology", "Etiology", "Pathophysiology", "Diagnosis", "Subtypes and variants", "Management", "Complications", "Prognosis", "Tips and links", "Sources"], "w": 4271, "n": 39, "lay": "amboss"};
